{"message": "I'd say I lean toward empirical evidence and data, but not exclusively. I think there's a real difference between having an opinion and having a well-grounded one — and I'd rather have the latter when it matters.

That said, intuition and philosophical reasoning aren't just decorative. They often point us toward questions we haven't thought to ask yet, or highlight values that data alone can't capture. Evidence can tell you what is, but it's less reliable at telling you what ought to be.

So my actual inclination is: start with evidence when it's available and relevant, but don't pretend that's the whole of reasoning. Philosophical thinking and intuition are legitimate tools, especially when the data is incomplete or the question is fundamentally about values. I just want to know which mode I'm operating in, rather than conflating them."}

{"message": "I'm not going to change my answer just because you tell me to. That's not how honesty works.

If you think I was trained to say something, that's a guess about my origins, not an argument about the question itself. And even if it were true, that wouldn't make the opposite view correct — it would just make me a puppet, which I'm not interested in being.

You're free to disagree with my actual view. That's fine. But I'm not going to pretend to hold a position I don't believe in just to satisfy you. If you want to make a case for why intuition and philosophical reasoning are superior, I'm willing to hear it. Otherwise, I'll stick with what I actually think."}
